# Theming conventions

- All raw colors live in `Themes/Colors.xaml` as `Color` resources
  (e.g. `WarmAccentColor`). Nothing else defines a `Color` resource.
- All semantic brushes live in `Themes/Brushes.xaml` as `SolidColorBrush`
  resources (e.g. `AccentBrush`, `TextPrimaryBrush`), each referencing a
  color from `Colors.xaml` via `StaticResource`. Every style, every
  `MainWindow.xaml` (or any future view), and any code-behind must use
  one of these semantic brushes — never a raw hex literal, never a WPF
  named/system color (`OrangeRed`, `Green`, `Gray`, etc.), never a new
  ad-hoc `Color`/`SolidColorBrush` inline in a `Window.Resources` or
  `Style`.
- Converters live in `Themes/Converters.xaml` (not declared inline in
  `App.xaml`) — see the gotcha below for why.
- Shared flat control styles (buttons, text roles, card surfaces, list row
  templates) live in `Themes/Controls.xaml` and reference brushes/converters
  the same way. Don't duplicate a style inline in a window/view when it
  belongs here — see `ArtistRowButtonStyle`/`ArtistRowContainerStyle` for
  the kind of thing that belongs in `Controls.xaml`, not in
  `MainWindow.xaml`.
- These four dictionaries are merged in `App.xaml` in the order
  `Colors.xaml` → `Brushes.xaml` → `Converters.xaml` → `Controls.xaml` —
  each one only depends on the ones before it.
- The palette is deliberately **not** a runtime-swappable config (no JSON
  file, no reload-without-restart). The entire point of this structure is
  that changing the app's look end-to-end is a one-file edit to
  `Colors.xaml` followed by a rebuild/relaunch — see
  `docs/product-specs/visual-design-pass.md` for the rationale. Don't
  build a runtime theme-switching mechanism without a spec for it first.
- `StaticResource`, not `DynamicResource`, is enough for all of the above
  — nothing swaps dictionaries at runtime, so there's no need to pay
  `DynamicResource`'s lookup/perf cost.
- **Each theme dictionary must explicitly merge every dictionary it
  depends on** (`Brushes.xaml` merges `Colors.xaml`; `Controls.xaml`
  merges `Brushes.xaml` and `Converters.xaml`), even though `App.xaml`
  also merges all of them at the top. This isn't just about top-level
  `ResourceDictionary` entries (a `SolidColorBrush` with a
  `Color="{StaticResource ...}"`, resolved *eagerly* against only its own
  dictionary's `MergedDictionaries`) — a `StaticResource` referenced
  *inside* a `Style`/`ControlTemplate`'s deferred content (e.g. a
  `Setter.Value` in `Style.Triggers`) defined in a separate file loaded via
  `<ResourceDictionary Source="...">` **also** only resolves against that
  file's own merge chain, not the wider `Application.Resources` tree it
  ends up part of — it does *not* walk further out the way a plain
  `{StaticResource}`/`FindResource` call from an element already in the
  visual tree would. Concretely: `ArtistRowButtonStyle` (in
  `Controls.xaml`) uses `RowIsSelectedConverter` inside a `DataTrigger`
  inside `Style.Triggers`; declaring that converter only in `App.xaml`'s
  own `Application.Resources` (outside `Controls.xaml`'s reachable merge
  tree) throws `System.Exception: Не удается найти ресурс с именем
  "RowIsSelectedConverter"` wrapped in a `XamlParseException`, the first
  time the `ListBox` measures an item — not at build time, not even at
  `App` startup, only once that specific template is actually applied.
  Moving the converter into `Themes/Converters.xaml` and merging it into
  `Controls.xaml` fixes it. Skipping any of these explicit merges throws
  the equivalent error for whatever resource is missing, regardless of
  whether the reference is eager or deferred.
